🔍 PREDICT THE OUTPUT — Switch Fall-Through:

int month = 2;
switch (month) {
    case 1: System.out.println("January");
    case 2: System.out.println("February");
    case 3: System.out.println("March");
    default: System.out.println("Quarter 1");
}

back.content:
Output:
February
March
Quarter 1

😱 Fall-through! Once a case matches, execution CONTINUES into ALL subsequent cases until a break or end of switch.

Fixed version:
switch (month) {
    case 1: System.out.println("January"); break;
    case 2: System.out.println("February"); break;
    case 3: System.out.println("March"); break;
    default: System.out.println("Quarter 1"); break;
}
// Now only prints: February

✅ Intentional fall-through (grouping cases):
case 1:
case 2:
case 3: System.out.println("Q1"); break; // 1,2,3 all go here

💡 Always add break unless you intentionally want fall-through.

while → Checks condition BEFORE executing body
do-while → Executes body FIRST, checks condition AFTER (runs at least once)

Example:
int attempts = 0;
do {
    // show PIN prompt — runs at least once even if attempts starts at 0
    System.out.println("Enter PIN (Attempt " + (++attempts) + ")");
    // read PIN...
} while (!pinCorrect && attempts < 3);

💡 When to choose do-while:
1. Menu-driven programs (show menu, then validate)
2. Input validation loops (get input, then check it)
3. Retry logic (ATM PIN, login attempts, network retry)

Key insight: If the body must run before any condition check is meaningful → use do-while
